'''tucker'''<sup>3</sup> (tuk′ėr), ''v. t.'' [Appar. < ''tucker''<sup>2</sup>, the phrase ''tucker out'' being appar. equiv. to ''ravel out.''] To tire; weary; cause to be tired or exhausted: commonly in the phrase ''tuckered out'', as a fish by struggling on the hook. [New Eng.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Hard work is good an’ wholesome, past all doubt;
But ’taint so ef the mind gits ''tuckered out.''</poem>
{{right|''Lowell'', Biglow Papers, 2d ser., ii.}}}}

